graphs=[
('Library visits',chart('Weekly library visits',['Week 1','Week 2','Week 3','Week 4'],[120,160,150,210],kind='line',unit='Visits'), 'Describe the overall pattern and compare the first and final weeks. Do not suggest causes.', 'Library visits increased overall during the four weeks, despite a small interruption in the upward movement. The total rose from 120 in Week 1 to 210 in Week 4, an increase of 90 visits, or 75%. Visits initially climbed by 40 to 160 in Week 2. They then fell slightly to 150 in Week 3 before reaching the highest recorded value of 210 in the final week. The last increase, of 60 visits, was larger than the first increase. Week 1 recorded the lowest total. Overall, the final figure was 1.75 times the starting value, while the only decline occurred between the second and third observations. The graph records visits rather than distinct visitors, so it does not show how many individual students used the library.'),
('Travel choices',chart('Travel to campus, 200 respondents',['Bus','Walk','Cycle','Car'],[80,60,40,20],unit='Respondents',kind='pie'), 'Compare travel choices and calculate the combined share walking or cycling. Do not explain the choices.', 'The survey shows that buses were the most common way of reaching campus, although walking and cycling together accounted for half of all respondents. Of the 200 people surveyed, 80 travelled by bus, representing 40%. Walking was the second most common choice, with 60 respondents or 30%. Cycling accounted for 40 people, equivalent to 20%, while cars were used by the remaining 20 respondents, or 10%. The number walking or cycling was therefore 100 out of 200, giving a combined share of 50%. Bus users outnumbered car users by 60 and were four times as numerous. Walking attracted 20 more respondents than cycling. These figures describe reported travel choices within the sample; they do not establish the reasons for those choices or whether the pattern applies to the entire campus.'),
('Support response times',chart('Median response time by support channel',['Email','Chat','Phone'],[18,6,10],unit='Minutes',kind='table'), 'Identify the fastest channel and compare it numerically with the others. Do not discuss service quality.', 'Chat had the shortest median response time among the three support channels shown. Its median was 6 minutes, compared with 10 minutes for phone support and 18 minutes for email. Thus, chat was 4 minutes quicker than phone support and 12 minutes quicker than email on this measure. Phone support occupied the middle position: its median was 8 minutes below the email figure but 4 minutes above the chat figure. The email median was three times the chat median. Overall, the difference between the highest and lowest medians was 12 minutes. These values compare response speed only. They do not indicate whether a query was resolved, how satisfied users were or how much the time varied between individual cases. A median also does not imply that every user waited exactly that long.'),
('Water consumption',chart('Hostel water use',['Monday','Tuesday','Wednesday','Thursday','Friday'],[40,38,42,35,30],unit='Thousand litres',kind='line'), 'Describe the pattern, the maximum and the net change. Do not infer reasons.', 'Hostel water use declined overall from Monday to Friday, although the sequence included a midweek rise. Consumption started at 40 thousand litres on Monday and ended at 30 thousand litres on Friday. This was a net decrease of 10 thousand litres, or 25% of the Monday value. Tuesday recorded 38 thousand litres, followed by a rise of 4 thousand litres to the weekly maximum of 42 thousand litres on Wednesday. Use then dropped to 35 thousand litres on Thursday and fell further to the minimum of 30 thousand litres on Friday. The largest consecutive decline was the 7-thousand-litre fall between Wednesday and Thursday. Overall, the highest and lowest readings differed by 12 thousand litres. The graph describes quantities used; it does not identify occupancy levels or explain why demand changed.'),
('Training completion',chart('Training completed by department',['Design','Support','Sales','Operations'],[85,90,70,80],unit='Percent of department'), 'Rank the departments and report the largest gap in percentage points.', 'Support had the highest training completion rate at 90%, followed by Design at 85%, Operations at 80% and Sales at 70%. All four departments therefore recorded completion rates of at least 70%. The largest gap was between Support and Sales, at 20 percentage points. Design exceeded Operations by 5 percentage points and Sales by 15 percentage points. Operations, in turn, was 10 percentage points above Sales. The rates place Support first and Sales last, with Design and Operations between them. However, the chart does not give department sizes, so a higher percentage cannot be treated as a larger number of employees completing training. Nor can the four percentages be added to obtain an organisation-wide completion rate. The information supports a comparison of departmental proportions, with the clearest difference separating the highest and lowest departments.'),
('Reading preferences',dict(kind='bar',title='Preferred reading format by study level',xLabel='Study level',yLabel='Respondents',labels=['Year 1','Year 2','Year 3'],series=[dict(name='Print',values=[30,25,20]),dict(name='Digital',values=[20,25,30])]), 'Compare print and digital preferences across study levels. Describe categories without claiming a time trend.', 'The preferred reading format differed across the three study levels. Print was more popular among Year 1 respondents, the two formats were equally popular in Year 2, and digital reading led in Year 3. In Year 1, 30 respondents preferred print and 20 preferred digital material, a gap of 10. Year 2 recorded 25 respondents for each format. The Year 3 figures reversed the Year 1 relationship, with 20 choosing print and 30 choosing digital. Each level contained 50 respondents. Across the whole sample, both formats attracted 75 respondents, so neither had an overall numerical advantage. These are comparisons between groups at different study levels, rather than repeated observations of the same students. The chart therefore does not demonstrate that individual readers changed their preferences as they progressed through university.')]
for title,c,p,m in graphs:add('graph',title,p,m,chart=c,target=(120,150),focus=c['title'])
summaries=[
('Repairable equipment', '''A campus computer room previously replaced faulty devices as soon as users reported a problem. A new maintenance team now inspects each device before deciding whether repair is practical. The team records the fault, the expected repair cost and the safety checks required. Devices with serious safety problems are removed immediately. Other units return to service after testing. The team also tracks repeated failures so that future purchases can take reliability into account. The aim is to reduce unnecessary disposal while maintaining a dependable service. Students can report faults through a form that asks for the room and device number. The maintenance process therefore combines immediate decisions about individual devices with information that supports longer-term planning.''', 'Repair Before Replacement\n\nA campus maintenance team ==inspects faulty devices==, recording repair costs and safety requirements before deciding their future. Unsafe equipment is withdrawn, while repaired units are tested before reuse. Tracking ==recurring failures== also supports more reliable purchasing. The system seeks to ==reduce unnecessary disposal== without weakening service, linking individual fault reports with longer-term equipment planning.'),
('Reading for understanding', '''A reading group asked students to explain short texts to a partner instead of memorising definitions immediately. Before discussion, each learner wrote a sentence stating the central idea. Partners then compared their explanations and returned to the original text when their interpretations differed. The teacher clarified unresolved questions at the end. The activity did not remove the need to remember important terms. Instead, it placed recall after an initial attempt to understand relationships between ideas. Some students needed more time than others, so the teacher shortened the passage rather than rush the discussion. The purpose was to make accurate understanding the basis for later revision and independent study.''', 'Understanding Before Recall\n\nThe reading group prioritised ==understanding== by having students identify a text’s main idea, explain it to a partner and check disagreements against the source. Teachers resolved remaining difficulties and adjusted passage length to allow discussion. Memorising essential terms remained necessary, but ==recall== followed comprehension, preparing learners for accurate revision and independent study.'),
('Shade maintenance', '''A college planted trees beside exposed walking routes after consulting students about uncomfortable outdoor areas. The gardening team chose species suited to local conditions and checked that their roots would not obstruct nearby paths. Each planting site was assigned a maintenance schedule covering watering, protection and replacement of failed saplings. Students received updates on the project but were not expected to provide its routine labour. The college planned to inspect the routes during different seasons before judging success. Its approach recognised that planting numbers alone could not establish whether shade had improved. Long-term survival, safe access and the comfort of people using the routes were the intended measures of progress.''', 'Evaluating Campus Shade\n\nThe college selected ==suitable trees== for exposed routes and planned watering, protection and replacement for each site. Root placement and path access were considered, while routine maintenance remained an institutional responsibility. Seasonal inspections would assess ==survival, safe access and user comfort==, recognising that the number planted alone could not demonstrate improved shade.'),
('Clinic communication', '''A clinic introduced reminders to help patients manage appointments. People could choose text messages or telephone calls, and staff recorded each preference. Texts stated the date and time and provided a number for rearranging the visit. They did not request private medical information in a reply. The clinic reviewed failed deliveries each week and contacted affected patients through another agreed channel where possible. Staff also asked whether the wording was understandable. The review distinguished messages reaching a phone from messages actually being read. The service was therefore evaluated through both delivery records and patient experience, rather than by assuming that sending more reminders would automatically improve attendance.''', 'Accessible Appointment Reminders\n\nThe clinic offered ==text or telephone reminders== according to patient preference, including appointment and rescheduling information without requesting medical details. Staff checked failed deliveries and sought feedback on clarity. Evaluation combined ==delivery records and patient experience==, distinguishing a message reaching a device from its being read and avoiding assumptions about improved attendance.'),
('Meeting design', '''A project team found that regular meetings often ended without clear decisions. It began circulating a short agenda stating the purpose of each item and whether discussion, a decision or an update was needed. A rotating note-taker recorded agreed actions, responsible people and deadlines. Items requiring detailed individual work were moved outside the meeting. Team members could propose changes to the agenda in advance. After a month, the team reviewed whether actions were completed and whether participants had enough opportunity to contribute. The goal was not simply to shorten meetings. It was to use shared time more effectively while making decisions and responsibilities easier to follow.''', 'Meetings with Clear Outcomes\n\nThe team improved meetings through ==purposeful agendas==, advance contributions and records of actions, responsibilities and deadlines. Detailed individual tasks were handled separately. A later review examined completed actions and participation. The objective was ==effective shared work== and traceable decisions, rather than merely reducing the length of meetings.'),
('Community reading', '''A community centre offered reading sessions for children after families requested activities outside school hours. Volunteers received training and worked under staff supervision. The programme included stories, discussion and opportunities for children to choose books. Attendance was voluntary, and the organisers avoided using formal tests to compare participants. Families were invited to comment on the timing and accessibility of the sessions. Staff also noted which activities encouraged participation. These observations informed plans for the following term. The organisers viewed the programme as support for enjoyable reading rather than as a substitute for school teaching or a competition based on the number of books completed.''', 'Reading for Enjoyment\n\nThe centre ran ==voluntary reading sessions== with trained, supervised volunteers and varied activities that allowed children to choose books. Family feedback and observations of participation guided future planning. The programme supported ==enjoyable reading==, avoiding formal comparisons and book-count competitions while complementing, rather than replacing, school teaching.')]
for title,p,m in summaries:add('summary',title,'Summarise the passage to about half its length. Give a title and highlight keywords in your answer. Include no personal opinions or minor details.',m,passage=p,target=(round(len(p.split())*.45),round(len(p.split())*.55)),focus='main purpose, procedure and important qualifications')
report_specs=[
('Library noise','noise complaints in the library','library manager','30 students and two librarians','three weekday afternoons','18 students reported disruption near the entrance; observations recorded six group conversations in the quiet zone','Signs identifying the quiet zone were partly hidden behind a display','move signs to eye level and relocate the display','provide a discussion area near the entrance','library manager'),
('Canteen waste','avoidable food waste in the canteen','campus administrator','24 diners and three serving staff','five lunch services','16 diners reported that fixed portions were too large; two of five services ended with unopened surplus trays','Staff had no routine way to record requests for smaller portions','offer a smaller portion with the option of a refill','record demand and remaining portions after each lunch','canteen supervisor'),
('Shuttle delays','morning shuttle delays','transport coordinator','20 passengers and two drivers','five weekday mornings','three of five observed journeys arrived more than ten minutes late; 14 passengers reported uncertainty about departures','The displayed timetable differed from the sheet used by drivers','publish one timetable at stops and on the student portal','review the first morning departure using the recorded journey times','transport coordinator'),
('Portal access','inaccessible course documents','academic coordinator','12 students and three course assistants','one teaching week','eight of 20 sampled documents were image-only scans; four students using assistive tools could not search those files','The upload checklist did not include an accessibility check','replace image-only scans with accessible text documents','add an accessibility check before each upload','course coordinator'),
('Meeting overload','ineffective project meetings','department manager','15 team members and two project leads','four weekly meetings','three meetings ended without named action owners; ten participants reported repeated status updates','No agenda distinguished decisions from updates','circulate an agenda marking decisions required','record every action with an owner and deadline','project leads'),
('Refill station reliability','unreliable campus refill stations','facilities manager','25 students and two maintenance staff','six station inspections over one week','two of six stations were out of service; 17 students reported not knowing where to report a fault','Maintenance requests had no visible contact label or tracking number','display a fault-reporting contact on every station','log inspections and publish temporary alternative water points','facilities manager')]
def report_model(s):
 title,issue,audience,sample,period,finding,cause,action1,action2,owner=s
 return f'''Investigation into {issue.title()}

Executive Summary
This report examines {issue}. Interviews and observations identified a service problem and a weakness in the arrangements supporting it. Clearer procedures and a short monitored trial are recommended. The evidence describes the investigation period and should not be treated as a permanent measure of performance.

Introduction
The investigation was requested by the {audience} following concerns about {issue}. Its purpose was to establish the main difficulties and propose practical improvements. The review covered routine use during {period}; it did not attempt to assess every possible condition.

Methodology
The investigation combined interviews with {sample} and direct observations during {period}. Interview responses were grouped by recurring concern. Observation notes were checked against these themes to distinguish reported experiences from events directly recorded. Participation was voluntary, and individual respondents were not identified. The limited sample restricts generalisation.

Findings
{finding[0].upper()+finding[1:]}. {cause}. These observations identify both the immediate difficulty and a process requiring attention. They do not establish that every user had the same experience.

Conclusion
The evidence indicates that the current arrangements do not consistently meet users’ needs. The procedural weakness may contribute to the difficulty, although this brief investigation cannot establish its full effect. Improvement requires clear responsibility and follow-up rather than a single announcement.

Recommendations
The {owner} should {action1} within two weeks. The same office should {action2} during a four-week trial. At the end of the trial, the original observation procedure should be repeated and users invited to comment. Results should determine whether to retain or revise the changes.'''
def report_question(spec,id=None,pool='original'):
 title,issue,audience,sample,period,finding,cause,a1,a2,owner=spec
 p=f'Investigate {issue} and write a report for the {audience}. For this simulated investigation, use these supplied notes: interviews with {sample}; observations during {period}; {finding}; {cause}. Include title, abstract/executive summary, introduction, methodology, findings, conclusion and recommendations. You may propose feasible actions. Do not invent additional results.'
 kw=dict(target=(300,360),focus=issue,annotations=['The executive summary previews the issue, evidence and recommended direction.','Methods state participants, period, procedure and a limitation. Findings use only the supplied evidence.','The conclusion interprets cautiously. Recommendations assign ownership, a deadline and a review measure.'])
 if id:return writing(id,'report',title,p,report_model(spec),pool=pool,**kw)
 return add('report',title,p,report_model(spec),**kw)
for spec in report_specs:report_question(spec)
